instrument names. The model_id already encodes it (VersaWeigh/SlackConveyor/Teorema prefix). I'll skip attributes.\n\nWait — but VersaGP and Versa RxC also use the standard weighing system, and Versa RxM/Versa Chain use Slack Conveyor. The model_id prefix \"VersaWeigh\" for standard system models is slightly off since VersaGP/Versa RxC share it. Maybe prefix \"Standard\" instead: \"Standard Max=1200g\"? Hmm. Or name by construction: \"Versa (standard weighing system) Max=1200g\". I think clearer: prefix \"VS\" no... Let me use:\n\n- \"VersaWeigh 1200 g\" → but VersaGP same specs. The certificate's first table is under \"Characteristics of the instrument\" (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC, Versa RxM, Teorema collectively). Then alternatives modify. Strictly, the page-2 table describes the base instrument family. The Slack Conveyor is an alternative weighing system for VersaWeigh/VersaGP. Teorema is a separate construction.\n\nSo maybe model IDs: \"Base Max=1200g\", ... \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e≥0.1g\"... \"Teorema Max=800g\". I'll use \"VersaWeigh Max=1200 g\" for base since VersaWeigh is the primary designation, and note in label. Good enough. Actually let me use neutral: \"Standard system Max=1200g\"? I'll go with \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\" — matches \"tested dynamically (Tedea 1042 C3, capacity 10 kg)\" base. Fine.\n\nLet me finalize model IDs (no spaces in units for cleanliness):\n1. \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\"\n2. \"VersaWeigh Max=2400g\"\n3. \"VersaWeigh Max=4800g\"\n4. \"VersaWeigh Max=12000g\"\n5. \"VersaWeigh Max=24000g\"\n6. \"VersaWeigh Max=48000g\"\n7. \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e≥0.1g\" — hmm \"≥\" in JSON string is fine. Or \"e=0.1g\". Use \"SlackConveyor 300g e0.1\"? I'll use \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.1g\". Let me use the ≥ char; JSON supports Unicode. To be safe with parsers, ASCII: \"e>=0.1g\". OK.\n8. \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.2g\"\n9. \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.5g\"\n10. \"SlackConveyor Max=600g\"\n11. \"SlackConveyor Max=1500g\"\n12. \"SlackConveyor Max=3000g\"\n13. \"SlackConveyor Max=6000g\"\n14. \"SlackConveyor Max=15000g\"\n15. \"Teorema Max=800g\"\n16. \"Teorema Max=1600g\"\n17. \"Teorema Max=3200g\"\n\nNow model_level arrays (17 values each):\n\nmaximum_capacity, unit \"g\":\nVersaWeigh: 1200, 2400, 4800, 12000, 24000, 48000\nSlack: 300, 300, 300, 600, 1500, 3000, 6000, 15000\nTeorema: 800, 1600, 3200\n\nminimum_capacity, unit \"g\":\n35, 70, 140, 350, 700, 1400; 4.5, 10, 10, 20, 50, 100, 200, 500; 42, 84, 168\n\nscale_interval_e (minimum), unit \"g\": (value {min, max:null} per rule? That makes each entry value an object {min:0.5,max:null}. Hmm, model_level example shows plain numbers. For ≥ I'll use {min: x, max: null}.)\nVersaWeigh: 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20\nSlack: 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5\nTeorema: strings:\n- \"≥0.5 g (42-125 g); ≥1 g (125-250 g); ≥2 g (250-800 g)\"\n- \"≥1 g (84-250 g); ≥2 g (250-500 g); ≥5 g (500-1600 g)\"\n- \"≥2 g (168-500 g); ≥5 g (500-1000 g); ≥10 g (1000-3200 g)\"\n\nload_cell_emax, unit \"kg\":\n10, 20, 30, 100, \"100 or 200\", \"100 or 300\"; 2, \"2 or 3\", \"2-10\", 3, 10, 20, 35, 100; 30, 50, 100\n\nHmm \"2 to 10 kg\" → {min:2, max:10}. Use range object for SC3. \"2 kg, 3 kg\" → \"2 or 3\". OK.\n\nNow type_level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"XIII(1)\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"AC\"}\n- climatic_environment: {value: \"Non-condensing (closed)\"} — hmm this is humidity/condensation condition. attribute name \"climatic_environment_condensation\"? Keep \"climatic_environment\": string.\n- interfaces: {value: \"RS 232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel\"}\n- automatic_zero_setting_interval: from devices \"requests a zero setting at least every 11 min\" — that's a device description. Skip or include? The Teorema one is 7 min 25 s. Skip the 11-min one (it's a device behavior description, could include). I'll skip to reduce noise. Actually maybe include in description.\n\nconfig_level:\n1. maximum_number_of_scale_intervals: axis \"weighing_system\":\n   - {condition: \"standard weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\", value: 2400}\n   - {condition: \"Slack Conveyor weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxM, Versa Chain)\", value: 3000}\n   - {condition: \"Teorema\", value: 1600}\n2. power_supply_voltage: axis \"weighing_system\":\n   - standard: {min:115, max:250}, \"V\"\n   - slack: {min:115, max:230}, \"V\"\n   - Teorema: 230, \"V\"\n3. power_supply_frequency:\n   - standard: 50, \"Hz\"\n   - slack: {min:50,max:60}, \"Hz\"\n   - Teorema: {min:50,max:60}, \"Hz\"\n4. climatic_environment_temperature:\n   - standard: {min:0,max:40}, \"°C\"\n   - slack: {min:0,max:40}, \"°C\"\n   - Teorema: {min:5,max:40}, \"°C\"\n5. electromagnetic_environment:\n   - slack: \"E1 and E2\"\n   - Teorema: \"E1 and E2\"\n6. tare: axis \"weighing_system\": three strings as above.\n7. maximum_conveyor_speed:\n   - axis \"weighing_system\": standard: 1.2 \"m/s\"\n   Actually make it one config_level entry with mixed axis? The schema: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. If I have two different axes for same attribute, I need two entries. So:\n7. maximum_conveyor_speed, axis \"weighing_system\": [{condition \"standard weighing system\", value 1.2}]\n8. maximum_conveyor_speed, axis \"number_of_scale_intervals (Slack Conveyor)\": [{condition \"Min-100\", value 1.6}, {\"100-2000\", 2.0}, {\"2000-3000\", 1.6}]\n9. maximum_conveyor_speed, axis \"weighing_range (Teorema)\": [W1 1.6, W2 2.6, W3 2.6]\n10. minimum_sensitivity: axis \"weighing_system\": standard/slack \"1 µV/e\" (for other Emax values), Teorema \"0.33 µV/div\". Note: \"Other Emax values may be chosen provided the sensitivity is greater than 1 µV/e\" appears after table 1 and after slack tables; Teorema: \"greater than 0.33 µV/div\". Values: {min:1,max:null} unit \"µV/e\"; {min:0.33,max:null} unit \"µV/div\". Mixed units within one entry — awkward. Split into two entries or use two config entries with unit per value? Rule says values follow {condition, value, unit_symbol} — so unit_symbol can be per value! Good: config values can carry unit_symbol each.\n11. automatic_zero_setting_frequency, axis \"weighing_system\": [{condition \"Teorema\", value \"7 min 25 s\"}]. Hmm — actually is it Teorema-only? Reading again: page 6: \"The frequency of automatic zero-setting is 7 min 25 s.\" — this is in the Teorema/derived alternatives section. It follows Teorema tables and \"Derived alternatives\" heading comes after. Ambiguous. I'll attribute condition \"Teorema\" but... risk. Alternatively type_level. Hmm. The sentence sits between the Teorema speed table and \"Derived alternatives:\". I'll use condition \"Teorema\". Actually, let me re-read page 6 order:\n\n\"Page 5 ends with load cell conditions list. Page 6: '- ... The remote control cabinet houses... The instruments have the following technical characteristics. [Teorema tables] ... [speed table] ... The frequency of automatic zero-setting is 7 min 25 s. The software is as described in the certificate. The interfaces and peripheral devices are as described the certificate. Derived alternatives: ...'\"\n\nThis whole section is Teorema. Yes, condition Teorema.\n\nComponents:\n```json\n{\n  \"role\": \"load_cell (standard weighing system - VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1042 C3\", \"Tedea Huntleigh 1250 C3\"],\n  \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"capacity\": \"according to technical data table (Emax 10 kg to 300 kg)\",\n    \"compatibility_conditions\": \"R60 OIML Certificate of Conformity required; no digital output; nlc, Y, Z same or better than Tedea 1042 C3 10 kg; same design/material; no oil damper; NH-marked cells require R76 humidity testing\"\n  }\n}\n```\nHmm characteristics{} — the schema says characteristics{}. Values there — should they be structured? To be safe use simple strings. Actually maybe keep characteristics minimal: {\"tested_capacity\": \"10 kg\"}. And a \"notes\" — not in schema. I'll use characteristics with plain string values; acceptable.\n\nSecond: Vishay Celtron LPS (Slack Conveyor), tested capacity 2 kg.\nThird: Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3 (Teorema), tested capacity 30 kg.\n\nAlso could add \"weigh_engine\"/\"HMI\" as components? Software section mentions Weigh Engine and HMI. Components entries: role \"indicator/control and display unit (HMI)\", role \"weigh_engine\"? These are named. I'll add:\n- {\"role\": \"weigh_engine\", \"type_designations\": [], ...} hmm type_designations empty. Maybe skip. I'll include HMI software modules via matrix table and description. Skip these as components to avoid noise. Actually let me include one component: {\"role\": \"control_and_display_unit_hmi\", \"type_designations\": [\"HMI\"], \"characteristics\": {\"software_modules\": \"MIDModule.dll, DataProcessing.dll, VW_Controls.dll\"}}? Eh. Keep just load cells.\n\nSoftware as component? The cert identifies software V312. I'll mention in certified_type.description.\n\nRecommendation:\n{\"id\": \"R51\", \"edition\": 2006, \"amendment\": null, \"accuracy_classes\": [\"XIII(1)\"]}\n\nEdition \"2006(E)\" → 2006.\n\nTest report: {\"id\": \"P01088\", \"date\": null, \"pages\": 12, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}. Date not given — omit date or null. Schema shows date field; rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit date.\n\nCertificate object: number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, project_number. oiml_issuer_id — omit.\n\nmember_state: \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\"? Header says \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\". I'll use \"United Kingdom\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2014-07-28\" (ISO). The revision history says rev 2 date 28 July 2014, issue date 28 July 2014. Good.\n\nRevision history revision strings: \"1\", \"2\", and first issue... The ISSUE NO for first is the base number. I'll include:\n- {\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2009-07-22\", \"changes\": \"Certificate first issued.\"}\n- {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2012-01-25\", \"changes\": \"Versa GP, Versa RxC and Versa RxM models added; software options added.\"}\n- {\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2014-07-28\", \"changes\": \"Software section added, software options section replaced by Alternative software section; Teorema instrument and Derived alternative sections added; Remote cabinets section added; Certificate history added.\"}\n\nFootnotes: none defined → omit (empty array or omit). I'll omit.\n\nNow certified_type:\n- category: \"Checkweigher\" — R51 automatic checkweighing instrument. Use \"Checkweigher\".\n- type_designations: [\"VersaWeigh\", \"VersaGP\", \"Versa RxC\", \"Versa RxM\", \"Versa Chain\", \"Teorema\"]\n\nHmm — is Versa Chain official? \"The models Versa RxM and Versa Chain use this construction with the Slack Conveyor weighing system.\" Yes. Include.\n- module_designation: \"Not applicable\" (complete instrument).\n- description: summary.\n\nApplicant address: \"Strada Rivoltana km 6/7 Rodano (MI)\", \"20090\", \"Italy\". address_lines: [\"Strada Rivoltana km 6/7\", \"Rodano (MI)\", \"20090\", \"Italy\"]. The \"km 6/7\" then \"Rodano (MI)\". Fine.\n\nManufacturer: \"The applicant\" → name \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.\", same address.\n\nNow let me also double check power supply table 1: \"115 - 250 Va.c. 50 Hz\" → voltage 115-250 V AC, freq 50 Hz. Yes.\n\nTable slack: \"115 - 230 Va.c. 50/60 Hz\".\nTeorema: \"230 Va.c. 50/60 Hz\".\n\nClimatic standard/slack: \"0°C to +40 °C\" → {min:0,max:40}. Teorema \"5°C to +40 °C\" → {min:5,max:40}.\n\nNow write the description:\n\n\"Mains-powered automatic checkweighing instrument (Category X). Certified constructions/types: VersaWeigh and VersaGP (standard weighing system; VersaGP with integrated frame of adjustable height), Versa RxC (stainless steel construction with standard weighing system), Versa RxM and Versa Chain (stainless steel construction with Slack Conveyor non-powered weighing system), and Teorema (floor-standing frame conveyor with remote cabinet). Devices: initial zero-setting, zero-tracking, automatic alarm device active during automatic operation (requests zero setting at least every 11 min), pre-set tare (subtractive), multi-range operation, static calibration not accessible to the user, dynamic calibration accessible to the user and recorded, internal memory for batch reports, device that acts upon significant faults, screen check at power-up. Interfaces: RS 232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel. Software: general software V312 Version 37.00.01.xx with legally relevant module 'MID Module'; alternative software V312 Version 2.0 (37.00.02.xx) with options for multi-lane, intermittent checkweigher (static-weigh, Slack Conveyor only, Max ≤ 1500 g), data matrix, OEM and Teorema applications, plus dedicated versions 39.00.01.xx (Tare/Gross), 41.00.01.xx (Multiproduct), 42.00.01.xx (Twin). Other Emax values permitted provided sensitivity > 1 µV/e (Teorema: > 0.33 µV/div). Remote cabinets allowed for VersaWeigh and VersaGP if load cell connected via metal conduit.\"\n\nGood, comprehensive.\n\nNow, do I include \"label\" in model entries? Optional. I'll include label same as model_id? Skip label.\n\nLet me now also reconsider whether to include the transposed technical tables as matrix_tables. I decided yes for fidelity. Let me define them:\n\nmatrix_tables[0]: \"Technical data - VersaWeigh standard weighing system\"\ncolumns: [\"attribute\", \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=2400g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=4800g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=12000g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=24000g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=48000g\"]\nrows:\n{\"attribute\": \"maximum_capacity_g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\": 1200, ...: 2400, 4800, 12000, 24000, 48000}\n{\"attribute\": \"minimum_capacity_g\", ...: 35, 70, 140, 350, 700, 1400}\n{\"attribute\": \"minimum_scale_interval_e_g\", ...: 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20}\n{\"attribute\": \"load_cell_emax_kg\", ...: 10, 20, 30, 100, \"100 or 200\", \"100 or 300\"}\ndescription: \"Scale interval values are minimum permitted values (e ≥ value). Additional rows in certificate: maximum number of scale intervals n = 2400; tare = -500 e (single-range) / -500 e1 (multi-range); belt speed 1.2 m/s; climatic environment 0 °C to +40 °C non-condensing; power supply 115-250 V a.c. 50 Hz; accuracy class XIII(1).\"\n\nHmm including that description duplicates config_level. Fine, keeps table faithful.\n\nmatrix_tables[1]: Slack Conveyor, columns: [\"attribute\", \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.1g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.2g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.5g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=600g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=1500g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=3000g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=6000g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=15000g\"]\nrows: maximum_capacity_g: 300,300,300,600,1500,3000,6000,15000; minimum_capacity_g: 4.5,10,10,20,50,100,200,500; minimum_scale_interval_e_g: 0.1,0.2,0.5,0.2,0.5,1,2,5; load_cell_emax_kg: 2,\"2 or 3\",{2-10?}... For matrix table keep \"2-10\" string. rows values: 2, \"2 or 3\", \"2-10\", 3, 10, 20, 35, 100.\n\nmatrix_tables[2]: Teorema, columns: [\"attribute\", \"Teorema Max=800g\", \"Teorema Max=1600g\", \"Teorema Max=3200g\"]\nrows: max: 800,1600,3200; min: 42,84,168; scale_interval: strings; LC Emax: 30,50,100.\n\nmatrix_tables[3]: conveyor speed slack.\nmatrix_tables[4]: conveyor speed Teorema.\nmatrix_tables[5]: software modules.\n\nThat's thorough. This JSON is getting long but OK.\n\nNow write model_level arrays fully. 17 values each × 4 attributes = 68 value objects. Plus config_level ~15 entries. Fine.\n\nLet me write values with footnote markers: none. unit_id: null everywhere? Rule 3: \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\". For type_level values, schema shape includes unit_id. I'll include unit_symbol and unit_id: null. Hmm, that adds bulk. The strict rule 2 shape: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": [...]}. I'll include unit_id: null only where unit_symbol non-null? To keep it lighter, I'll include unit_symbol always and unit_id: null always (safe). Actually, I can omit unit_id when null? Rule says shape MUST include... I'll include unit_id: null consistently in type_level, and for model/config values follow the example pattern (no unit objects in entries). For type_level entries I'll give full {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers} but omit footnote_markers when empty? The shape shows footnote_markers in schema. I'll include footnote_markers: [] only when needed... To be consistent with \"MUST be object with this shape\", include all keys: value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers (empty array if none). Slightly verbose but compliant.\n\nHmm, for accuracy_class value \"XIII(1)\" — unit_symbol null, unit_id null, footnote_markers []. Note \"(1)\" looks like footnote marker but it's part of class designation in R51. Keep as is, no footnote marker extraction since no footnote defined.\n\nNow, some strings: \"100 or 200\" — I'll write \"100 or 200\". Similarly \"2 or 3\". And \"2-10\" as {min:2,max:10}. Hmm inconsistent with \"100 or 200\" string. For model_level load_cell_emax: values: [10, 20, 30, 100, {\"min\":100,\"max\":200}? No — \"100 or 200 kg\" means either/or, not range. Use string \"100 or 200\". For \"2 to 10 kg\" that IS a range → {min:2,max:10}. OK mixed types in one values array: numbers, strings, one range object. Acceptable.\n\nWait, actually for matrix table rows I wrote \"2-10\" string — keep consistent with model_level? In matrix table I'll use \"2-10\". Hmm, could use {\"min\":2,\"max\":10} in the table too. Tables: rows as plain objects — I'll use \"2-10\" string in matrix table (raw text preservation) and structured in model_level. Fine.\n\nConveyor speed slack: values 1.6, 2.0, 1.6.\n\nNow units: µV/e — use \"µV/e\" (micro sign). µV/div likewise. °C fine.\n\nInterfaces string: \"RS 232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel\".\n\nLet me also double-check table 1 Min capacities: 35, 70, 140, 350, 700, 1400 g. Yes matches (35=1200/2400*70? n=2400: 1200/2400=0.5 → min 35 g plausible with e... whatever, OCR given).\n\nMax capacities: \"1 200 g\", \"2 400 g\", \"48 00 g\" → 4800 g (OCR spacing error), \"12 000 g\", \"24 000 g\", \"48 000 g\". Good.\n\nScale interval e: \"≥ 0.5 g\" etc. e ≤ Max/2400: 1200/2400=0.5 ✓. 2400/2400=1 ✓. 4800/2400=2 ✓. 12000/2400=5 ✓. 24000/2400=10 ✓. 48000/2400=20 ✓. \n\nSlack n=3000: 300/3000=0.1 ✓; 600/3000=0.2 ✓; 1500/3000=0.5 ✓; 3000/3000=1 ✓; 6000/3000=2 ✓; 15000/3000=5 ✓. \n\nTeorema n=1600: 800/1600=0.5 ✓; 1600/1600=1 ✓; 3200/1600=2 ✓. Consistent.\n\nNow assemble final JSON.\n\nAlso \"project_number\": \"T1108/0053\" — I'll include under certificate.\n\ncertificate.scheme: omit (not stated).\n\nLet me think about whether to include \"oiml_issuer_id\": omit.\n\nIssuing authority email: not present — omit.\n\nPerson responsible: \"Paul Dixon – Product Certification Manager\" → name \"Paul Dixon\", title \"Product Certification Manager\". Signatory: \"P R Dixon for Chief Executive\". person_responsible: \"Paul Dixon\", person_title: \"Product Certification Manager\". OK.\n\nPhone: \"+44 (0)20 8943 7272\".\n\nWebsite: \"www.gov.uk/nmo\".\n\nNow let me write the JSON.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R51/2006-GB1-09.04\",\n    \"project_number\": \"T1108/0053\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"United Kingdom\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2014-07-28\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"National Measurement Office\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Paul Dixon\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Product Certification Manager\",\n    \"phone\": \"+44 (0)20 8943 7272\",\n    \"website\": \"www.gov.uk/nmo\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Strada Rivoltana km 6/7\", \"Rodano (MI)\", \"20090\", \"Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Strada Rivoltana km 6/7\", \"Rodano (MI)\", \"20090\", \"Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Checkweigher\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"VersaWeigh\", \"VersaGP\", \"Versa RxC\", \"Versa RxM\", \"Versa Chain\", \"Teorema\"],\n    \"module_designation\": \"Not applicable\",\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [ ... 17 ... ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [ 3 load cells ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"XIII(1)\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"AC\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"climatic_environment\": {\"value\": \"Non-condensing (closed)\", ...},\n      \"interfaces\": {\"value\": \"RS 232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel\", ...}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [ 4 entries ],\n    \"config_level\": [ entries ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [ 6 ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R51\", \"edition\": 2006, \"amendment\": null, \"accuracy_classes\": [\"XIII(1)\"]},\n  \"test_reports\": [{\"id\": \"P01088\", \"pages\": 12, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}],\n  \"revision_history\": [3],\n}\n\nWait — \"48 00 g\" issue: I'm confident it's 4800 g. Yes.\n\nOne more check: table 1 column 5 LC Emax: \"100 or 200 kg\", col 6: \"100 or 300 kg\". Yes.\n\nDevices list — put into description. Done above.\n\nConfig level — also \"automatic_zero_setting_frequency\" Teorema \"7 min 25 s\". Include.\n\nAlso \"tare\" config entries with strings.\n\nAlso include belt speed standard 1.2 m/s.\n\nAlso sensitivity entries.\n\nLet me carefully write config_level:\n\n[\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"standard weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\", \"value\": 2400},\n    {\"condition\": \"Slack Conveyor weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxM, Versa Chain)\", \"value\": 3000},\n    {\"condition\": \"Teorema\", \"value\": 1600}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"standard weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 115, \"max\": 250}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Slack Conveyor weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxM, Versa Chain)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 115, \"max\": 230}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Teorema\", \"value\": 230, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_frequency\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"standard weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Slack Conveyor weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxM, Versa Chain)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Teorema\", \"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"temperature_range\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"standard weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Slack Conveyor weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxM, Versa Chain)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Teorema\", \"value\": {\"min\": 5, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"electromagnetic_environment\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Slack Conveyor weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxM, Versa Chain)\", \"value\": \"E1, E2\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Teorema\", \"value\": \"E1, E2\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"tare\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"standard weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\", \"value\": \"-500 e (single-range instruments); -500 e1 (multi-range instruments)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Slack Conveyor weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxM, Versa Chain)\", \"value\": \"T <= 30% Gross for Gross <= 500e; T <= 50% Gross for 500e < Gross <= 3000e for speed <= 1.8 m/s; T <= 15% Gross for 500e < Gross <= 2000e for speed >= 1.8 m/s; T <= 50% Gross for 2000e < Gross <= 3000e (e1 replaces e for multi-range instruments)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Teorema\", \"value\": \"T <= 30% Gross\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_conveyor_speed\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"standard weighing system (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\", \"value\": 1.2, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_conveyor_speed\",\n  \"axis\": \"number_of_scale_intervals (Slack Conveyor weighing system)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Min-100\", \"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"100-2000\", \"value\": 2.0, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"2000-3000\", \"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_conveyor_speed\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_range (Teorema)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"W1\", \"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"W2\", \"value\": 2.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"W3\", \"value\": 2.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_required_sensitivity_for_alternative_load_cell_emax\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"standard and Slack Conveyor weighing systems\", \"value\": {\"min\": 1, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": \"µV/e\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Teorema\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.33, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": \"µV/div\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"automatic_zero_setting_frequency\",\n  \"axis\": \"weighing_system\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Teorema\", \"value\": \"7 min 25 s\"}\n  ]\n }\n]\n\nNote: use ≤/≥ Unicode? JSON supports. I'll use \"≤\" and \"≥\" characters for readability — they're fine in JSON. The markdown uses \"<=\" escapes. I'll use ≤, ≥, <. Keep ASCII-safe? JSON with UTF-8 is fine. I'll use ≤ and ≥.\n\nModel level entries:\n\n[\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_capacity\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"g\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\", \"value\": 1200},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=2400g\", \"value\": 2400},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=4800g\", \"value\": 4800},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=12000g\", \"value\": 12000},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=24000g\", \"value\": 24000},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=48000g\", \"value\": 48000},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.1g\", \"value\": 300},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.2g\", \"value\": 300},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.5g\", \"value\": 300},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=600g\", \"value\": 600},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=1500g\", \"value\": 1500},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=3000g\", \"value\": 3000},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=6000g\", \"value\": 6000},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=15000g\", \"value\": 15000},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=800g\", \"value\": 800},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=1600g\", \"value\": 1600},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=3200g\", \"value\": 3200}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_capacity\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"g\",\n  \"values\": [35, 70, 140, 350, 700, 1400, 4.5, 10, 10, 20, 50, 100, 200, 500, 42, 84, 168 mapped]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"scale_interval_e_minimum\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"g\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.5, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=2400g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 1, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=4800g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 2, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=12000g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 5, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=24000g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 10, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=48000g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 20, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.1g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.2g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.2, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.5g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.5, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=600g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.2, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=1500g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.5, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=3000g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 1, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=6000g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 2, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=15000g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 5, \"max\": null}},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=800g\", \"value\": \"≥0.5 g (42-125 g); ≥1 g (125-250 g); ≥2 g (250-800 g)\"},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=1600g\", \"value\": \"≥1 g (84-250 g); ≥2 g (250-500 g); ≥5 g (500-1600 g)\"},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=3200g\", \"value\": \"≥2 g (168-500 g); ≥5 g (500-1000 g); ≥10 g (1000-3200 g)\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"load_cell_emax\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\", \"value\": 10},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=2400g\", \"value\": 20},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=4800g\", \"value\": 30},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=12000g\", \"value\": 100},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=24000g\", \"value\": \"100 or 200\"},\n    {\"model\": \"VersaWeigh Max=48000g\", \"value\": \"100 or 300\"},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.1g\", \"value\": 2},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.2g\", \"value\": \"2 or 3\"},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.5g\", \"value\": {\"min\": 2, \"max\": 10}},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=600g\", \"value\": 3},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=1500g\", \"value\": 10},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=3000g\", \"value\": 20},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=6000g\", \"value\": 35},\n    {\"model\": \"SlackConveyor Max=15000g\", \"value\": 100},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=800g\", \"value\": 30},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=1600g\", \"value\": 50},\n    {\"model\": \"Teorema Max=3200g\", \"value\": 100}\n  ]\n }\n]\n\nAttribute name \"scale_interval_e_minimum\" — canonical label rule: scale_interval_e? The rule 1 example: `Max. number of load cell intervals n_LC` → `max_load_cell_intervals_nlc`. So e: \"scale_interval_e\"? I'll use \"scale_interval_e\". And values as {min,...} to indicate minimum. Good.\n\nComponents:\n\n[\n {\n  \"role\": \"load_cell (standard weighing system: VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC)\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1042 C3\", \"Tedea Huntleigh 1250 C3\"],\n  \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"tested_capacity\": {\"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"},\n    \"alternative_load_cells\": \"Any compatible load cell with a valid OIML R60 Certificate of Conformity; not digital output; nlc, Y, Z same or better than Tedea 1042 C3 10 kg; same design and material; no oil damper; NH-marked load cells only with R76 humidity testing\",\n    \"capacity_selection\": \"Load cell Emax per capacity variant: 10 kg / 20 kg / 30 kg / 100 kg / 100 or 200 kg / 100 or 300 kg\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"role\": \"load_cell (Slack Conveyor weighing system: VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxM, Versa Chain)\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"Vishay Celtron LPS\"],\n  \"alternatives\": null,\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"tested_capacity\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"},\n    \"alternative_load_cells\": \"Any compatible load cell with a valid OIML R60 Certificate of Conformity; not digital output; nlc, Y, Z same or better than Vishay Celtron LPS 2 kg; same design and material; no oil damper; NH-marked load cells only with R76 humidity testing\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"role\": \"load_cell (Teorema)\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3\"],\n  \"alternatives\": null,\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"tested_capacity\": {\"value\": 30, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"},\n    \"alternative_load_cells\": \"Any compatible load cell with a valid OIML R60 Certificate of Conformity; not digital output; nlc, Y, Z same or better than Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3 30 kg; same design and material; no oil damper; NH-marked load cells only with R76 humidity testing\"\n  }\n }\n]\n\nMatrix tables — write out.\n\nTable A rows (keys = column names). Let me write:\n\n{\n \"name\": \"Technical data - VersaWeigh standard weighing system\",\n \"columns\": [\"attribute\", \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=2400g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=4800g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=12000g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=24000g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=48000g\"],\n \"rows\": [\n   {\"attribute\": \"maximum_capacity_g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\": 1200, \"VersaWeigh Max=2400g\": 2400, \"VersaWeigh Max=4800g\": 4800, \"VersaWeigh Max=12000g\": 12000, \"VersaWeigh Max=24000g\": 24000, \"VersaWeigh Max=48000g\": 48000},\n   {\"attribute\": \"minimum_capacity_g\", ...: 35, 70, 140, 350, 700, 1400},\n   {\"attribute\": \"scale_interval_e_min_g\", ...: 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20},\n   {\"attribute\": \"load_cell_emax_kg\", ...: 10, 20, 30, 100, \"100 or 200\", \"100 or 300\"}\n ],\n \"description\": \"Values per Max capacity variant; scale interval values are minimum permitted (e >= value). Further fixed data for this construction: maximum number of scale intervals n = 2400; tare = -500 e (single-range) / -500 e1 (multi-range); belt speed 1.2 m/s; climatic environment 0 °C to +40 °C non-condensing (closed); power supply 115-250 V a.c. 50 Hz; accuracy class XIII(1). Other load cell Emax values allowed if sensitivity > 1 µV/e.\",\n \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nTable B similar with 8 columns.\n\nTable C Teorema:\nrows include scale_interval strings:\n{\"attribute\": \"scale_interval_g\", \"Teorema Max=800g\": \"≥0.5 (42-125 g); ≥1 (125-250 g); ≥2 (250-800 g)\", ...}\n\nSpeed tables, software table.\n\ncolumn_units: schema shows column_units in R137 example. My tables have attribute-rows so column_units = [null, \"g\", \"g\", \"g\", ...]? Units differ per row (last row kg). I'll include column_units [null, \"g\",\"g\",\"g\",\"g\",\"g\",\"g\"] with note that load_cell_emax row is kg. Or omit column_units. Schema lists columns[] and rows[]; column_units appeared in the example but not the schema yaml. I'll include column_units where uniform; for attribute-row tables omit or set nulls. I'll omit column_units for the transposed tables and include for speed/software tables where columns are homogeneous:\n- speed tables: column_units: [null, \"m/s\"]\n- software: column_units: [null, null, null]\n\nOK. Also footnotes: [] — omit if empty? Schema shows footnotes[] optional. I'll include only where non-empty; none are. Omit.\n\nLet me also decide whether to include \"scheme\" in recommendation: The R51 certificate under old scheme — omit.\n\nAccuracy class: \"XIII(1)\". Hmm wait — R51 accuracy classes are actually \"X\", \"XI\", \"XIII(1)\", \"XIII(2)\"? R 51-1 defines accuracy classes X, XI, XIII (checkweighers), with XIII subdivided? Whatever — reproduce as-is.\n\nLet me also reconsider: \"Identification of the certified pattern: VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC, Versa RxM and Teorema Checkweighers\". Versa Chain appears only in description. Including it in type_designations is reasonable. Yes.\n\nNow description field — include devices and software info. Write it compactly.\n\nAlso note \"Category X\" operation.\n\nFinal JSON assembly. Be careful with valid JSON: no trailing commas, escape nothing weird. Use Unicode chars directly (°, ≥, ≤, µ, ³ not needed here). Should be fine.\n\nOne more consideration: certificate.scheme — the layered schema wants scheme (A|B). Not in doc → omit. project_number \"T1108/0053\" — labelled \"Reference No\". Hmm, could be the certificate project. I'll include as project_number.\n\ndate_issued: \"2014-07-28\".\n\nPage total: 7.\n\nLet me now write out the complete JSON.\n\nAlso double-check slack table min capacities: 4.5, 10, 10, 20, 50, 100, 200, 500 g. Yes.\n\nTeorema min: 42, 84, 168. Yes.\n\nCheck \"0.33 µV/div\" — yes \"sensitivity is greater than 0.33 µV/div\" for Teorema.\n\nSoftware: general V312 Version 37.00.01.xx; MID Module legally relevant; version breakdown 37 standard, 00 custom, 01 major, x minor. Alternative V312 2.0 (37.00.02.xx). Dedicated: Tare/Gross 39.00.01.xx; Multiproduct 41.00.01.xx; Twin 42.00.01.xx.\n\nInclude in description.\n\nWrite description:\n\n\"Mains-powered automatic checkweighing instrument (Category X). Constructions: VersaWeigh and VersaGP with the standard weighing system (VersaGP with integrated frame and adjustable-height supports); VersaWeigh and VersaGP alternatively with Slack Conveyor non-powered weighing system; Versa RxC in stainless steel construction with the standard weighing system; Versa RxM and Versa Chain in stainless steel construction with the Slack Conveyor weighing system; Teorema with floor-standing frame conveyor and remote electrical cabinet with HMI. Devices: initial zero-setting, zero-tracking, automatic alarm device active during automatic operation (zero setting requested at least every 11 min), pre-set tare (subtractive), multi-range operation, static calibration not accessible to the user, dynamic calibration accessible to the user and recorded, internal memory for batch reports, device that acts upon significant faults, screen check at power-up. Interfaces: RS 232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel. Software: general software V312 Version 37.00.01.xx, legally relevant module 'MID Module' (shown in Menu/Tools/Versions); alternative software V312 Version 2.0 (37.00.02.xx) with selectable options for multi-lane, intermittent checkweigher (static-weigh; restricted to Slack Conveyor systems and Max <= 1500 g), data matrix, OEM and Teorema applications, and dedicated versions for Tare/Gross (39.00.01.xx), Multiproduct (41.00.01.xx) and Twin (42.00.01.xx) applications. Other load cell Emax values may be chosen provided sensitivity > 1 µV/e (Teorema: > 0.33 µV/div). Remote cabinets permitted for VersaWeigh and VersaGP provided the load cell is connected to the cabinet via metal conduit.\"\n\nGood.\n\nNow, do I include \"label\" in model entries? Optional. I'll include label same as model_id? Skip label.\n\nLet me now also reconsider whether to include the transposed technical tables as matrix_tables. I decided yes for fidelity.\n\nLength is large but acceptable. Let me write final answer carefully as pure JSON.\n\nCareful with \"µ\" char and \"≥\", \"≤\", \"°C\" — all fine.\n\nOne concern: in model IDs I use \"e>=0.1g\" — maybe better \"e>=0.1g\". Keep ASCII to be safe. Actually JSON handles ≥ fine, but ASCII safer for downstream. Use \"e>=0.1g\".\n\nModel ids final:\n\"VersaWeigh Max=1200g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=2400g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=4800g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=12000g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=24000g\", \"VersaWeigh Max=48000g\",\n\"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.1g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.2g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.5g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=600g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=1500g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=3000g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=6000g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=15000g\",\n\"Teorema Max=800g\", \"Teorema Max=1600g\", \"Teorema Max=3200g\"\n\nWrite it all out now.",
